WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — gates (90s) Narrator George

1
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.531
First, a computer should not take an irreversible

2
00:00:03.531 --> 00:00:07.069
action until an independent check confirms it is safe.

3
00:00:07.569 --> 00:00:11.005
Second, it should try the cheapest method first and

4
00:00:11.005 --> 00:00:14.442
wake a large AI model only when simpler tools miss.

5
00:00:14.942 --> 00:00:19.779
Third, every decision is written to a receipt with the energy it used,

6
00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:21.782
so anyone can audit it later.

7
00:00:22.282 --> 00:00:27.497
Think of how a hospital pharmacy or airplane cockpit separates suggesting an

8
00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:32.505
action from executing it. An AI model can propose whatever clever step it

9
00:00:32.505 --> 00:00:38.268
likes, while an independent deterministic gate holds the sole key to external tools.

10
00:00:38.768 --> 00:00:44.265
Instead of waking a massive neural network on routine tasks, our cascade tries

11
00:00:44.265 --> 00:00:50.045
table lookup first, then simple formulas, then exact solvers, and finally a model,

12
00:00:50.045 --> 00:00:56.176
stopping the instant a check passes or refusing cleanly when remaining budget runs low.

13
00:00:56.676 --> 00:01:02.370
Across more than two hundred nine thousand simulated control checks, the discrete safety

14
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:08.322
gate allowed zero unsafe actions. On real programmable hardware with an inline circuit-board

15
00:01:08.322 --> 00:01:15.051
power sensor, one whole-module gate cycle measured eleven thousand four hundred twenty-eight picojoules.

16
00:01:15.551 --> 00:01:22.965
Model-generated suggestions are never silently relabeled as guaranteed rules, and every allowed or refused step

17
00:01:22.965 --> 00:01:29.243
writes its exact reason and labeled energy source to a permanent receipt for later inspection.
